def s(title,*paragraphs): return (title,list(paragraphs))
SECTIONS={
'strength-standards-calculator':[
s('What a strength standard describes',
'A strength standard compares a named performance with a particular reference. In this tool, the performance is a one-rep maximum divided by body weight. The reference is a set of published aggregate bodyweight-ratio bands from Strength Level. The output is deliberately narrower than a claim about how strong every person of your age should be. It does not assess movement quality, health, athletic skill or the work you have done to reach the number.',
'The source population matters. People who log lifting results are not a random sample of everyone who could lift. The labels describe that lifting context. A beginner label can include someone with extensive experience in another sport, and an advanced label on one lift says little about an unfamiliar movement. Read the label as a reference for this specific input rather than as an identity.',
'The simplified ratio method also differs from a detailed weight-class table. A fixed ratio produces a straight-line target when multiplied by body weight. Real performance does not necessarily scale in a straight line across all body sizes. Use the bands for a broad coaching comparison and use the relevant competition rules or detailed source tables when a more specific comparison is needed.'),
s('Check the lift before entering a maximum',
'Choose the exercise that matches the completed lift. A back squat is not a leg press, a bench press is not a machine press, and a standing overhead press is not a seated dumbbell press. Substituting a different exercise can produce a convincing number with the wrong interpretation. Keep variations separate in your log and compare like with like.',
'Agree on range and technique. Squat depth, pauses on the bench, deadlift lockout and assistance can change the performance. A heavier partial repetition is not directly comparable with a full repetition under another standard. If you change the execution intentionally, record it as a new variation or make the difference clear beside the result.',
'A maximum can be tested or estimated. An estimate from a recent repetition set avoids requiring a maximal attempt, but it introduces uncertainty. A set performed far from failure, an unfamiliar lift or a change in technique makes the estimate harder to interpret. If the result is close to a band boundary, do not treat a tiny change as a decisive promotion.'),
s('Read the next threshold as a reference',
'The next threshold is the load corresponding to the next ratio at the body weight you entered. It is not a load to attempt in your next session. The calculator does not know your current working sets, recent training, equipment or recovery. Those details determine how a coach might turn a broad benchmark into a practical plan.',
'At 180 lb body weight, a 275 lb squat produces a ratio of about 1.53. Using the published male squat ratios in this tool, that is above the Novice threshold of 1.25 and below Intermediate at 1.75. Multiplying 180 by 1.75 gives 315 lb. The forty-pound gap describes the benchmark difference, not a promise about how quickly it can be closed.',
'If body weight changes, the threshold changes too. A higher ratio can come from a larger lift, a lower body weight or both. Do not assume weight loss is the right way to improve the label. The appropriate goal depends on the person, their health and their training. Track the actual lift and body weight alongside the ratio so the reason for a change remains visible.'),
s('Use separate standards for separate lifts',
'The four exercises have different mechanics and typical loading. A deadlift ratio should not be used to judge a bench press. Select the correct lift before reading the band. If one result looks much lower than another, consider practice, technique, exercise selection and training emphasis before deciding that it represents a problem.',
'An overhead press often progresses in smaller absolute increments than a squat or deadlift. Plate availability can make those increments awkward. Keep a realistic working progression rather than letting a broad benchmark push you into a jump you cannot control. The next category is a reference point; consistent sessions and sound technique are the work that make progress possible.',
'Differences between lifts can help organise a conversation. A client may practise the bench often while doing little overhead work. That explains part of the comparison without needing a diagnosis of imbalance. Review the training record before adding more volume to the lower-ranked movement, because changing frequency, technique practice and total work all have consequences.'),
s('Turn a benchmark into a reviewable goal',
'Begin with the recent sessions rather than the category name. Write down the exercise, normal working loads, repetitions, effort and any technical issue the coach is addressing. Decide which observation would count as progress over the next block. More controlled repetitions at the same load may be more relevant than a new estimated maximum on a day with altered technique.',
'Choose a review date without guaranteeing a result by that date. At the review, inspect attendance, completed work and whether the movement became more repeatable. A goal can remain useful even when the estimate has not crossed the next boundary. Conversely, crossing a threshold once does not prove that a new working weight is appropriate every session.',
'Keep the calculation consistent. Use the same unit for maximum and body weight, the same sex category and the same source version. If the source changes later, distinguish a recalculated benchmark from an actual change in performance. Preserving those details avoids a misleading before-and-after comparison.'),
s('What the calculator leaves out',
'Age, training age, disability, injury history, limb proportions, equipment and technique are not inputs. The output therefore cannot adjust for them. Do not read an age-specific expectation into a result simply because another standards website offers age tables. This implementation uses the published aggregate ratios described in its method note.',
'The result is not a percentile of the general population. It does not say that you are stronger than a particular percentage of all people, and it does not determine whether someone is healthy enough to train. It is also not a competition qualifying total. Federations use their own divisions, rules and qualifying standards.',
'Use a coach or relevant professional when the decision requires more than a comparison. Returning after injury, selecting a maximal attempt or planning weight change needs individual context. A benchmark can help organise that discussion, but it cannot replace it.')],
'weekly-training-volume-calculator':[
s('Count a week before judging a session',
'A session can look modest while the week contains a large amount of repeated work. Five chest sets on four days add to twenty, even though no single day appears unusually long. The calculator makes that multiplication visible for each muscle group. This helps when a split spreads work across several sessions or when new accessories have accumulated over time.',
'Use the actual week you intend to perform. If you train a muscle twice on some weeks and three times on others, record those weeks separately or calculate a clearly labelled average. An average can be useful for a repeating rotation, but it should not conceal a very different workload on individual weeks. The tool assumes the same entered set count for every listed session.',
'If sessions differ, add the sets directly before interpreting the result. Six sets on Monday and four on Thursday produce ten weekly sets. Entering five sets across two sessions represents that total, but the distribution still matters. Keep the actual session details in your plan, because the calculator summarises quantity rather than reconstructing the whole split.'),
s('Treat volume landmarks as adjustable assumptions',
'MEV, MAV and MRV are coaching terms for minimum effective, maximum adaptive and maximum recoverable volume. The default values are landmarks for comparing a total, not measurements taken from your body. Entering twenty as MRV does not establish that twenty-one sets are unsafe or that nineteen are guaranteed to be recoverable.',
'The result bands are mechanical. Below MEV means the total is below your first entered landmark. Productive lies from MEV to MAV, High lies above MAV through MRV, and Above MRV exceeds the last value. Those names help organise the plan, but the calculator has no information about technique, effort, sleep, stress or whether performance is improving.',
'Keep the landmarks in order and review them with your coach. If you change them simply to turn every row into a reassuring colour, you remove the point of the audit. Choose a consistent framework, record the assumption and compare it with the work you actually complete over several sessions. The tool supports that review; it does not discover personal physiological limits.'),
s('Decide which sets you are counting',
'Working sets, warm-up sets and easy technique rehearsal serve different purposes. Use a consistent convention for hard working sets so the weekly total remains interpretable. If you count every empty-bar warm-up as equivalent to a demanding set, the arithmetic will be correct but the comparison will not mean what you intended.',
'Effort matters too. A set stopped very early and a set close to the planned repetition limit are not identical stimuli merely because both contain ten repetitions. This tool counts sets; it does not weight them by effort. Keep the target and reported effort in the session record when using a weekly total to make training decisions.',
'Indirect work needs an explicit convention. Rows involve elbow flexors and presses involve triceps, but that does not make every compound set equivalent to a direct isolation set. You can track direct work here and keep overlapping work as context, or use another consistent coach-agreed approach. Avoid changing the counting rule midway through a comparison.'),
s('Audit distribution before adding more work',
'A weekly total does not show whether all the sets occur on one day or are spread across several. Distribution affects how the session feels, how much time it needs and how well later sets can be performed. When a client struggles to finish the final exercises, review session structure rather than looking only at whether the weekly number is below a landmark.',
'Exercise order also matters. Direct arm work after several pulling or pressing movements happens under different fatigue than the same exercise performed first. If you move it, establish a new reference rather than treating the immediate load increase as proof that more volume was required. Quantity and context need to be read together.',
'Look for duplicated work introduced by separate changes. A new upper-body day, an extra arm exercise and an optional weekend session may each look reasonable individually. Together they can alter the week substantially. Recount after changing the plan and confirm which optional sessions the client actually attends.'),
s('Use performance and experience to review the totals',
'Compare several recognisable sessions. Ask whether loads or repetitions are progressing at the intended effort, whether technique remains consistent, and whether the client can complete the schedule. A weekly total is one part of that review. It should not automatically overrule a client who reports that the current plan is not manageable.',
'Change one variable deliberately. If you add sets, preserve enough of the exercise selection and execution to understand what happens next. Simultaneously adding frequency, sets and harder effort makes it difficult to identify why performance or recovery changed. A clear record makes the next adjustment easier to explain.',
'A lighter week or a reduced schedule changes the interpretation of the audit. Do not compare it with a normal training week as though the reduction were an accidental failure. Label planned changes and review whether they served their purpose. The calculator can summarise either week, but the coach supplies the reason behind it.'),
s('Keep weekly sets distinct from other volume measures',
'Some logs use load multiplied by repetitions as a volume measure. Others use total repetitions or session duration. These are different summaries. This calculator uses weekly set counts for named muscle groups and does not compute tonnage. A heavier load can increase tonnage without adding a set, while more sets can raise this result even if total load lifted changes differently.',
'Do not compare a set-count target with a tonnage target as if their units matched. Write the measure explicitly when communicating with a client or another coach. Clear units prevent a phrase such as increase volume from becoming several different instructions at once.',
'The result should lead back to the session plan. Confirm what the client will do, which sets count, how effort is described and when the plan will be reviewed. That is more useful than pursuing a weekly total without a clear connection to the completed work.')],
'workout-plan-builder':[
s('Make the schedule decision first',
'Choose days you can attend repeatedly, including the time needed for travel, setup and recovery between exercises. The most detailed plan is not useful if it assumes a schedule that rarely happens. Start with a realistic frequency, then distribute the work across those days. This builder supports two through six sessions and changes the split accordingly.',
'Training days are not necessarily consecutive calendar days. The output numbers sessions in sequence, leaving you and your coach to place them in the week. Consider work, other sport and the demands of neighbouring sessions before assigning dates. A six-session template should not be interpreted as a requirement to train hard every day indefinitely.',
'If your schedule varies, agree on how to resume after a missed session. Continuing with the next planned session can be more practical than cramming several workouts into the remaining days. Keep the completed record so your coach knows which work happened. A missed day is information for planning, not a reason to double everything without review.'),
s('Understand which split the builder selects',
'Two days uses full body A and B. Three days adds full body C. These sessions combine upper- and lower-body movements so the plan does not depend on a separate day for every body part. A lower frequency can still provide a clear structure, provided the exercises and workload suit the person using it.',
'Four days uses upper A, lower A, upper B and lower B. Five days combines an upper/lower pair with push, pull and legs. Six days repeats push, pull and legs twice. These are fixed template choices, not conclusions from an assessment. Review the resulting weekly work rather than assuming a longer split is automatically more appropriate.',
'The template does not ask about available equipment, injuries, session length or exercise preferences. It includes movements using a typical gym setup. If you do not have that equipment, adapt the exercise list with your coach before starting. Do not assume an unfamiliar machine or free-weight lift can be substituted without changing the intended movement and load.'),
s('Read goal-specific sets and repetitions',
'The muscle-building option uses four sets of six to ten repetitions for main lifts and three sets of ten to fifteen for accessories before the experience adjustment. The strength option uses five sets of three to five on main lifts and three sets of six to ten on accessories. General fitness and fat loss use three sets of eight to twelve on main lifts and three sets of twelve to fifteen on accessories.',
'These are template rules, not a claim that a repetition range serves only one goal. The goal selection provides a starting emphasis. Loads still need to match technique, experience and intended effort. The builder does not calculate a weight for each exercise because it has no recent performance data for you.',
'General fitness adds a short bike or rower interval suggestion; fat loss adds a brisk incline-walking suggestion. Neither guarantees a body-weight outcome. Adapt or omit these additions when your equipment, capacity or other activity makes them unsuitable. A coach should review the whole week rather than treating an optional finisher as the defining part of the plan.'),
s('Use experience as a modest template adjustment',
'For beginners, the builder removes the last accessory and subtracts one main-lift set, with at least two remaining. Intermediate keeps the base template. Advanced adds one main-lift set. This is a transparent adjustment to the starting workload, not a validated classification of readiness based on years spent in a gym.',
'Choose experience in the context of the movements you will perform. Someone returning after a long break may need a more conservative plan despite years of earlier training. A person experienced in one sport may still be learning these lifts. The dropdown cannot replace that context, so review the output honestly rather than selecting Advanced as a badge.',
'More sets are not automatically a better use of an experienced person’s time. Technique practice, exercise order, load and the capacity to recover all matter. If the template becomes impractical, reduce or adapt it with your coach. The output is intended to be understandable enough that those changes can be made deliberately.'),
s('Turn the list into a session you can follow',
'Name the exact exercise and show a demonstration when needed. Specify whether repetitions are a target range, whether warm-up sets are separate and what to do when equipment is unavailable. A list of exercise names is only the beginning. Clients need to understand how to perform the session and how to report the completed work.',
'Write rest and effort in ordinary terms. This builder suggests longer rests for strength-focused main lifts and a shorter general range for other goals. The correct practical rest still depends on the exercise and person. Avoid rushing a difficult set solely to fit a timer when that compromises technique or the planned effort.',
'Use recent sessions to choose a starting load. If there is no reliable reference, begin conservatively and use the first sessions to establish one. Do not derive a maximal attempt from the template’s set count. A coach can help select an appropriate load and adjust it after reviewing actual repetitions and execution.'),
s('Review the first week before changing the whole plan',
'After the first week, compare the planned sessions with what happened. Identify unavailable equipment, confusing cues, exercises that took longer than expected and loads that did not fit the target. Fix those practical issues before deciding that the entire split needs replacement. A usable plan becomes better through specific feedback.',
'Keep a stable core long enough to understand performance. Replacing every exercise each week makes progress hard to interpret. You can change a movement when there is a clear reason, but record the change and establish a new baseline. Consistency makes the training log informative rather than a list of unrelated workouts.',
'Choose a review rule with your coach. It might involve completed repetitions at a consistent effort and technique, rather than automatically adding weight every week. Progress is not guaranteed to follow a fixed calendar. A plan should explain the next decision when a session is easier, harder or incomplete, so the client knows how to continue.')],
'injury-exercise-swap':[
s('Start with the decision to stop or seek assessment',
'An exercise that hurts is not simply a search problem for another exercise name. First stop the painful movement and consider whether professional assessment is needed. This tool cannot examine an injury or distinguish one cause of pain from another. Choosing a body area does not provide enough information to diagnose what happened or clear a different movement.',
'The painful-under-load and cannot-load selections therefore withhold loaded alternatives. They direct you toward assessment instead of giving a recovery plan based on a dropdown. Sudden severe symptoms, inability to bear weight or new numbness or weakness need prompt medical advice through the services available where you live. Do not use a calculator result to delay care.',
'If a clinician has already provided a plan, follow that guidance. General suggestions should not override a restriction, progression rule or return-to-training decision given for your situation. Tell your coach what guidance you have and what remains unclear before changing the session.'),
s('Compare movement patterns rather than assuming equivalence',
'The tool groups the selected exercise into a broad pattern such as pressing, pulling, squatting or hinging. For a mild concern, it lists alternatives with relevant equipment. A matching pattern can help a coach organise options, but it does not mean the replacement stresses the same tissues in the same way or will be comfortable for you.',
'A floor press limits the available lowering range compared with a bench press. A chest-supported row changes the support compared with a bent-over row. Those are practical differences to discuss, not guarantees of pain relief. No exercise in this list is universally safe for an injured joint or entirely free of spinal or joint loading.',
'Do not copy the previous exercise’s load directly onto a substitute. Machines, cable ratios, movement ranges and body positions differ. If an alternative is appropriate after review, it needs its own starting point. The purpose of the list is to support that conversation, not to produce a conversion between exercises.'),
s('Use the equipment filter literally',
'Select the equipment actually available for the session. Dumbbells, bands, bodyweight and cable or machine options describe different practical setups. Full gym makes a wider list available. When no option in the selected pattern matches your equipment, the tool says so rather than suggesting equipment you do not have.',
'A bodyweight option can still require a suitable support, anchor or surface. Check the setup before trying it. A band exercise needs an appropriate secure anchor, and an inverted row needs equipment that can support the task. The equipment label is not a complete installation or safety check.',
'If the available setup is unsuitable, ask your coach for another way to organise the session. It may be better to omit the movement temporarily than to improvise an unstable version. Do not let a desire to complete every exercise override a clear practical limitation.'),
s('Do not turn a suggestion into a rehabilitation schedule',
'This tool does not assign a percentage increase each week or promise a return date. Recovery differs across conditions and people, and the inputs here cannot establish a safe loading schedule. A fixed sequence of sixty, seventy-five and ninety percent would imply individual knowledge the tool does not have.',
'A goal such as building muscle does not make training through pain appropriate. In particular, do not take an unfamiliar alternative to failure around a painful area because it appears on a list. Discuss effort, range and load with the relevant professional. Stop if symptoms appear or worsen rather than completing the prescription at any cost.',
'Keep the original movement and the substitute separate in the log. Record what was attempted, what was comfortable and what prompted stopping. Those observations can help a coach or clinician understand the session. They are not a self-diagnosis, and they should not be used to overrule a professional restriction.'),
s('Keep communication specific',
'Tell your coach which movement caused the concern, when it occurred, whether it was sudden and whether normal activities are affected. Avoid reducing the whole situation to a number out of ten. A single pain score cannot explain the cause or indicate whether an exercise is appropriate.',
'When a replacement is agreed, put the exact movement and the agreed limitations in the plan. A vague instruction such as do something easier leaves too much interpretation to the client at the gym. Make it clear whether the movement is omitted, modified or awaiting assessment, and provide a way to ask questions before the next session.',
'The useful outcome is continuity with appropriate boundaries: a client understands what to do next, the coach knows what happened and medical decisions remain with someone able to assess the person. Completing every planned exercise is not the priority when a movement is painful.')]
}
SECTIONS['strength-standards-calculator'] += [
s('How to read each lift in context',
'For the back squat, establish a consistent depth and setup before comparing ratios. A change in bar position, footwear or range can alter the load you can lift. The category does not tell you whether the repetition met your intended standard. Keep that technical standard in the plan, and use a video or coach review when you are unsure whether recent performances are comparable.',
'For the bench press, distinguish a paused repetition from touch-and-go work and note any assistance. A spotter helping through the final portion changes what was lifted independently. Use a recent unassisted maximum or estimate from a clearly completed set. The reference does not know whether a repetition was paused, whether the hips stayed on the bench or whether the range changed.',
'For the deadlift, keep the variation consistent. A conventional deadlift, sumo deadlift, Romanian deadlift and trap-bar pull are different movements. Select Deadlift here only for the matching barbell lift you intend to compare. A higher starting position or straps can alter the performance, so preserve those details in your log rather than assuming the ratio explains the whole result.',
'For the overhead press, use the same strictness convention each time. Leg drive can turn a strict press into a push press. Sitting on a supported bench also changes the task compared with a standing barbell press. If the movement changes, the resulting maximum belongs to a different baseline. A lower number with a stricter standard can be better information than a higher ambiguous one.'),
s('Two useful ways to track progress',
'First, track the actual performance: a maximum, or a recent set recorded with load, repetitions and effort. This preserves the task you completed. Second, track the bodyweight ratio used by the calculator. Together they distinguish a stronger lift from a change in the denominator. If you only retain the category name, you lose that distinction and make later decisions harder.',
'Imagine the same 275 lb squat recorded at 180 lb and then at 175 lb body weight. The ratios are approximately 1.53 and 1.57. The ratio rose even though the lift did not change. That may be relevant to a particular goal, but it does not show an increase in the load lifted. Keep both observations instead of describing every higher ratio as an increase in absolute strength.',
'Now imagine increasing the squat from 275 to 285 lb while staying at 180 lb. The ratio moves from about 1.53 to 1.58. That is a clear increase in load under the assumed same technique, even though both results remain inside the same band. The broad category hides a useful improvement, which is why it should not be the only measure of progress.'),
s('Review a result close to a boundary',
'An estimated maximum can vary enough to cross a nearby threshold without a meaningful change in training. A different formula, a slightly different effort rating or rounding can move the estimate. If the result sits near the next band, confirm the input and look for repeated comparable performance before making a large programme change.',
'The next-load display is rounded for readability. It is not a plate-loading instruction. A theoretical threshold might fall between the increments your equipment supports. Use the plate-loading tool to understand available loads, then decide on a session target from the planned repetitions and effort. Do not attempt an awkward or excessive load just to clear a label.',
'A result well inside a band is not a reason to train without review either. The same category can contain a wide range of experience, and progress within the category still matters. A coach can choose a smaller performance target that is appropriate for the current block rather than treating the next threshold as the only worthwhile outcome.'),
s('Use a source you can identify later',
'The source and checked date are part of the result’s meaning. This calculator uses published aggregate bodyweight ratios checked on 15 November 2026. It does not claim that those ratios are a new population study or that they reproduce every detailed table on the publisher’s site. The linked source pages let you inspect the context and compare the simplified method with other presentations.',
'If you bring a result from another calculator, compare methods before comparing labels. One site may use age groups, another may use competition results and another may use fixed ratios. Intermediate on one scale does not necessarily mean the same thing on another. Ask which lift, source population, units and thresholds were used before interpreting a difference as a mistake.',
'For a training group, keep one documented method during a block. If you change it later, record that change explicitly. A new label after a formula update is not the same event as a new personal best. Good records preserve enough context that a client can understand why the number or category changed without guessing.')]
SECTIONS['weekly-training-volume-calculator'] += [
s('Review the upper-body rows',
'Chest: count the planned chest-focused working sets across the week and check how they are distributed. Bench pressing, incline pressing and fly variations may all appear on different days. Confirm which movements you are counting directly. If you add an optional pressing session, update the weekly total instead of continuing to read the old split as though nothing changed.',
'Back: pulling work can include different patterns with different fatigue and setup demands. Six sets across four sessions produce twenty-four weekly sets in the default example. That single number does not distinguish rows from pulldowns or reveal whether demanding deadlifts also appear nearby. Review the exercise list before deciding that every set serves the same purpose or costs the same amount of recovery.',
'Shoulders: identify the direct shoulder work separately from overlapping pressing. Four sets on three days produce twelve weekly sets. If you count pressing toward another row as well, write that convention down so the totals remain understandable. Do not treat twelve direct sets plus considerable overlapping work as identical to twelve isolated sets in an otherwise different programme.',
'Biceps: three sets on three days produce nine weekly direct sets in the default row. Rows and pulldowns may add indirect work. The calculator does not estimate the size of that contribution. When elbows become uncomfortable or performance falls, look at the entire pulling schedule rather than adding curls simply because the direct-set row falls below a chosen landmark.',
'Triceps: three sets across three sessions also produce nine sets. Check the amount and order of pressing in those sessions before interpreting that total. Isolation work performed after hard bench and overhead pressing is not the same context as triceps work performed first. The arithmetic is useful, but exercise order and overlap explain more of the actual experience.'),
s('Review the lower-body and trunk rows',
'Quads: five sets on three days produce fifteen weekly sets. Check whether squats, leg presses and split squats have been included under a consistent rule. A session containing several of those exercises can become lengthy even when each individual prescription looks modest. Consider the time and technique demands of the complete session before increasing the weekly number.',
'Hamstrings: four sets across two sessions produce eight weekly sets. Hinge work and knee-flexion work are different patterns, even when both involve the same muscle group. The total does not tell you whether the plan includes both or whether one movement is unfamiliar. Use the exercise record alongside the number when reviewing the next block.',
'Glutes: three sets on two days produce six direct sets in this summary. Squatting and hinging may also involve the glutes, depending on execution and exercise. Do not automatically conclude that six means the muscle receives only six sets of relevant work. Decide what the row is intended to represent and retain that definition across reviews.',
'Calves: three sets twice weekly produce six sets. Repetition range, range of motion, exercise choice and effort still matter. Recording only a weekly count can conceal that the client is using a different machine or shortening the movement. Keep a clear execution cue and a completed log so a change in the total can be interpreted against stable work.',
'Abs: four sets twice weekly produce eight direct sets. Other exercises also require trunk work, but this row does not quantify it. Do not combine every squat or carry with direct trunk sets under an unstated counting rule. If a coach chooses to track several forms of trunk training, label them clearly rather than treating them as interchangeable repetitions.'),
s('A practical audit after a schedule change',
'Suppose a client changes from four sessions to three but keeps the same exercises. Recalculate the weekly sets before simply compressing the old schedule. Moving all the work into fewer days preserves quantity on paper but can create longer, harder sessions. The first task is to decide which work remains important and feasible, not to preserve every old number at any cost.',
'If the client adds a sport practice or a physically demanding job shift, that work will not appear in these muscle rows. It can still affect the week. Ask about the broader schedule and interpret changes in performance with that context. The absence of an activity from the calculator does not mean it has no effect.',
'After an interruption, avoid using the old weekly total as an automatic return target. Review current capacity, recent sessions and any professional guidance. This tool has no return-from-injury or detraining model. You can enter a reduced week and compare it with a later one, but the progression between them needs an individual decision.'),
s('Keep the audit actionable',
'End with a specific decision that can be reviewed. You might remove duplicated accessory work, spread sets more evenly, retain the plan while collecting better effort notes, or reduce the session length to fit attendance. Each decision has a reason that should be visible in the record.',
'Keep the earlier totals when changing the plan. Overwriting them loses the comparison that explains why the new block differs. A simple record of sets, sessions and landmark assumptions is enough to distinguish a deliberate change from a data-entry mistake. Revisit the actual sessions after the change rather than assuming the revised table alone proves the problem is solved.')]
SECTIONS['workout-plan-builder'] += [
s('A two- or three-day full-body starting point',
'Full body A starts with a back squat and bench press, then includes a barbell row, Romanian deadlift and plank before the beginner adjustment. This combines several movement patterns in one session. Check that the client knows the exercises and has the equipment before treating the list as ready to use. The builder cannot inspect technique or decide which variation best suits an individual.',
'Full body B starts with a deadlift and overhead press, followed by a lat pulldown, walking lunge and cable crunch. Those exercises require different setup and skills from day A. Review the transition between stations and how much time the session is likely to take. A workable sequence matters as much as a complete list of movement patterns.',
'The three-day version adds full body C: front squat, incline dumbbell press, seated cable row, hip thrust and farmer’s carry. A beginner version omits the last accessory. The plan therefore changes with both frequency and experience. Read the actual generated sessions rather than assuming that adding a day simply repeats the first one.',
'Place the sessions in a realistic week with your coach. The numbered days express sequence, not fixed weekdays. If a session is missed, agree on how the sequence continues. Avoid squeezing two demanding sessions together just to preserve the original calendar when a simpler adjustment would keep the work more consistent.'),
s('A four-day upper and lower split',
'Upper A includes bench press and barbell row as main movements, followed by overhead press, lat pulldown, biceps curl and triceps pushdown before adjustments. Upper B uses incline dumbbell press and pull-up as main movements, then seated dumbbell press, seated cable row, lateral raise and hammer curl. This provides different sessions rather than two identical upper-body days.',
'Lower A includes back squat and Romanian deadlift as main movements, with leg press, leg curl and standing calf raise. Lower B starts with deadlift and front squat, then walking lunge, hip thrust and seated calf raise. Review how these sessions interact with other activity. The template does not know whether the client also runs, plays sport or has a demanding physical schedule.',
'The four-day selection can make each appointment more focused, but it still requires four attendances. If the client consistently completes only two or three, reconsider the schedule rather than letting the missing days become a permanent gap. A simpler frequency that is completed may be more useful than a split whose lower-body sessions are repeatedly missed.',
'Use the weekly volume calculator to inspect the selected work, applying a consistent rule for direct and overlapping sets. Do not assume the split name alone establishes an appropriate workload. The exercise list, sets, effort and actual attendance determine what the client does.'),
s('Five and six days require a review of the whole week',
'The five-day option combines upper A and lower A with push, pull and legs. The six-day version repeats push, pull and legs twice. These higher-frequency choices are convenient templates, but frequency itself is not proof of readiness. Read the weekly set count and consider whether the client can repeat the sessions with the intended technique and effort.',
'The push session includes bench press and overhead press, then incline dumbbell press, lateral raise and triceps pushdown. Pull includes deadlift and pull-up, then barbell row, face pull and biceps curl. Legs includes back squat and Romanian deadlift, then leg press, leg curl and standing calf raise. The ordering can produce substantial work across neighbouring sessions.',
'Check recovery and scheduling rather than assuming a six-day selection is a more serious version of the same plan. A client may have ample training experience but limited time or additional sport demands. Another may prefer short frequent sessions. Those individual reasons matter more than the label in the dropdown.',
'If the generated work is too much, adapt it deliberately with your coach. Do not simply perform every main lift to failure while skipping the remaining exercises unpredictably. A revised plan with clear priorities produces a more interpretable log and gives the client a better instruction for the next attendance.'),
s('Write the rules for an incomplete or unexpectedly difficult session',
'Clients need to know what to do when the day differs from the plan. An exercise station may be occupied, a load may feel unusually hard, or available time may be shorter. Agree on priorities and communication before those situations occur. The builder does not choose an automatic response for every interruption.',
'For a load that misses the target repetitions, record what was completed and the effort rather than editing the log to match the prescription. The coach can then decide whether to repeat, reduce or change the target. An honest incomplete set is more useful information than a completed checkbox with no explanation.',
'For time-limited sessions, identify which work is essential and which can be omitted after discussion. Do not turn every missed accessory into extra work at the next appointment. The next session already has its own demands. A clear plan for interruptions helps the client continue without improvising large changes.',
'When pain or concerning symptoms occur, stop the relevant movement and seek appropriate advice. The template is not a medical assessment and does not make an exercise appropriate for every person. Keep that boundary clear even when the exercise appears in a familiar split.'),
s('Use a stable record to guide the next block',
'After several comparable sessions, review more than the largest weight lifted. Look at attendance, completed repetitions, technique, effort and whether instructions were understood. If the client follows the plan consistently, you have a better basis for a small progression. If practical problems dominate the record, solve those before adding complexity.',
'Preserve the original prescription alongside the completed work. This lets a coach distinguish a load that was intentionally reduced from one entered incorrectly. Keep notes short and specific, such as different machine or shortened session, so the next review does not depend on memory.',
'The builder provides structure, while the training record supplies individual evidence. Use both. The goal is not to keep regenerating a new split every time progress slows, but to understand the current plan well enough to make the next useful change.')]
SECTIONS['injury-exercise-swap'] += [
s('Understand the limits of each pattern',
'A horizontal press alternative changes features such as grip, support or available depth. A vertical press alternative changes the pressing angle or body position. Neither category identifies which tissue is irritated. A movement that is comfortable for one person may provoke symptoms for another, even when their selected body area is the same. Use the list to ask a better question, not to certify safety.',
'Squat alternatives may change support, depth or where an external load is held. Hinge alternatives may change the starting position or direction of resistance. These changes can alter the task, but none makes the exercise automatically unloaded. Avoid interpreting bodyweight as zero stress or a machine as a guarantee that a joint will be protected.',
'Pulling alternatives can change handle orientation, support and whether the arm moves overhead. Elbow-flexion and elbow-extension alternatives can change grip and resistance. The tool ranks some options by the selected area, but that is a broad organisational rule. It does not model an injury or know which range and load are appropriate for the person.'),
s('Keep the first discussion small and specific',
'If a coach or clinician agrees that trying an alternative is appropriate, clarify the exercise, setup and stopping rule before the session. Do not combine several new movements, a larger workload and a harder effort target while trying to understand one concern. Too many changes make it difficult to tell what happened.',
'Write down any symptoms that occur during or after the attempt and bring those observations back to the relevant professional. The absence of discomfort during one set does not establish that the injury has recovered. This tool has no follow-up assessment and cannot determine readiness for the original movement.',
'If the client is unsure about the instruction, leave the movement out until it is clarified. A missed exercise is easier to review than an improvised attempt made under pressure to complete the plan. Make it clear that reporting a problem is useful information, not a failure to follow the programme.'),
s('Use medical guidance without turning the tool into a diagnosis',
'The NHS guidance on sprains and strains explains when an injury needs prompt help, including difficulty using or bearing weight on the affected area. It is a general reference, not a way to identify your condition from these inputs. Follow the appropriate local care pathway and ask a qualified clinician when symptoms or the situation are concerning.',
'For the next coaching session, record the practical status: awaiting assessment, a clinician-approved modification, or a movement currently omitted. Avoid assigning a diagnosis in the training log unless it comes from an appropriate assessment. This keeps the plan useful while respecting the limits of a coaching tool.',
'The same boundary applies when using an AI assistant or a search engine after seeing the result. More text about an exercise does not create an individual examination. Keep questions focused on understanding the agreed plan and the equipment setup, and take medical questions to someone qualified to assess them.')]
SECTIONS['strength-standards-calculator'].append(s('Explain the result without making a promise',
'When sharing a result, include the lift, maximum, body weight, unit and whether the maximum was tested or estimated. Add the source method and the current band. This is enough context for another coach to understand the calculation without guessing. A screenshot containing only the word Intermediate loses most of the useful information.',
'Use the next threshold to frame a question: what training would help this lift progress while fitting the client’s actual schedule? That question invites a plan grounded in recent work. Saying the client must reach the next level within four weeks would add a deadline the calculator cannot justify.',
'If the client is disappointed by a label, bring the discussion back to their own record. A first consistent month, improved technique or a repeatable set can be meaningful progress before a broad band changes. The category is a reference that should support the training discussion, not replace the client’s reason for training.'))
SECTIONS['weekly-training-volume-calculator'].append(s('Check the entries before interpreting a flag',
'Confirm whether each number means sets in one session or sets across the whole week. Entering a weekly total in the per-session field and then multiplying it by frequency counts the work twice. For example, ten sets split across two days could be represented as five sets per session and two sessions. Entering ten and two would report twenty instead.',
'A zero can be a deliberate choice. A muscle group may have no direct work in the week being reviewed, or you may be counting its involvement elsewhere under an agreed convention. A blank entry is different: it means the input is incomplete. Finish the entry before using the output as evidence for a plan change.',
'Inspect the landmarks as well as the rows. Accidentally entering a larger minimum than maximum makes the comparison incoherent. Keep MEV, MAV and MRV in increasing order and write down the reason if you change them. The numbers are editable so a coach can apply a documented framework, not so a misleading flag can be hidden.',
'Finally, compare the audit with the completed record. Optional sessions may not have happened, an exercise may have been substituted, or sets may have been stopped early. The planned total and actual total can both be useful, but they answer different questions. One describes the intended block; the other describes work performed.',
'Use the gap to improve the next instruction. If a client repeatedly misses the final exercises because the appointment runs out of time, adding more planned sets will not solve it. Review priorities, session length and attendance first. A smaller completed plan is easier to assess than a larger table whose totals rarely reflect the week.'))
SECTIONS['workout-plan-builder'].append(s('A final checklist before the first session',
'Confirm the exact exercise variations and the equipment available. Check that the client understands the set and repetition notation, which sets are warm-ups, and how the target effort should feel. Make sure they know how to ask about an unfamiliar exercise before loading it. The builder does not supply an individual movement assessment.',
'Confirm the time available for the complete session, including rest and setup. A session with five exercises can take very different amounts of time depending on equipment changes and rest between demanding sets. If the schedule is tight, revise the plan in advance rather than expecting the client to rush the final half.',
'Confirm the reporting rule. Record completed loads and repetitions, not just whether the session was opened. Add a short note when the exercise or equipment changed. This gives the coach something concrete to review and helps prevent the same confusion from appearing in the next session.',
'Confirm when the plan will be reviewed and what to do before then if it is too easy, too hard or incomplete. The review date is a point to examine evidence, not a guaranteed date for a heavier lift or a particular body-weight result. A client should understand how to continue while the coach uses the log to make the next adjustment.',
'These checks turn a template into an understandable starting plan. Keep them visible and concise in the session instructions. More explanation is helpful only when it resolves a real question; the client should be able to recognise the next exercise and follow it without searching through separate messages.'))
SECTIONS['injury-exercise-swap'].append(s('Record why an exercise is currently omitted',
'A clear note prevents a later session from restoring a painful movement by accident. State that the movement is awaiting assessment or that a modification has been agreed, without adding a speculative diagnosis. Keep any clinician’s restrictions available to the coach and client.',
'When the plan changes again, review that note rather than assuming the passage of time alone means the original exercise is suitable. The tool does not track recovery or issue clearance. Returning to a movement should follow the individual guidance and context that justified the change, with a new record of what was actually performed.'))
SECTIONS['weekly-training-volume-calculator'].append(s('Retain the assumptions for the next review', 'Save the counting convention and landmark values with the week you reviewed. When you return later, use those same assumptions before deciding whether the plan changed. Otherwise a new counting method can look like a large training increase even when the client performed exactly the same work.'))
